lesson:
```
char grade(int score)
{
    if (score >= 80) return 'A';
    else if (score >= 65) return 'B';
    else if (score >= 50) return 'C';
    return 'F';
}
```

In C, **0 is false and everything else is true**. `bool` from `<stdbool.h>` is just a neat name for that; `true` is 1 and `false` is 0, so `printf("%d", true)` prints `1`.

```
int alarms = 3;
if (alarms) printf("check the tank\n");   /* runs, because 3 isn't 0 */
```

`switch` chooses between fixed whole-number or char values:

```
switch (cmd) {
    case 'p': pump_on(); break;
    case 's': pump_off(); break;
    default:  printf("unknown\n");
}
```

Two classic bugs: `if (x = 0)` (assigns, never true) and forgetting `break` (falls into the next case). `gcc -Wall` warns about the first one; Archie's checker turns warnings on.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

int main(void)
{
    int level = 8;
    bool alarm = level < 10 || level > 95;
    printf("alarm: %d\n", alarm);
    if (level) printf("tank is not empty\n");
    char cmd = 'p';
    switch (cmd) { case 'p': printf("pump on\n"); break; default: printf("?\n"); }
    return 0;
}

